.TH EUCLID-CLI-EES 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ees \- subscribe to the events euclid modules publish
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B ees
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ees
module lets something outside euclid \- an application, an SDK, a shell
script \- receive the events euclid's modules publish among themselves,
so it can react to a change instead of polling for it.
.PP
The events themselves are named
.IR producer . object . change ,
and the ones that exist today come from
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
esm.object.created   an object appears at a key that held none
esm.object.updated   a key is re\-uploaded, or an attribute changes
esm.object.deleted   an object is removed
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every path that changes an object publishes one \- an upload, a
multipart completion, a copy, a move, a rename, an attribute change, a
delete, and a purge, which publishes one event per object it removed. A
move is a
.I created
followed by a
.IR deleted ,
in that order, so a subscriber keeping its own index of keys never has
the object missing from both places.
.SH SUBSCRIPTIONS
A subscription is a
.I name
plus an event type plus an optional filter. The name is what makes this
different from a queue: events are stored per subscriber, so
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
nothing is lost while a consumer is down \- an event waits until it is
claimed, and is removed only when it is acknowledged, so restarting an
application does not miss what arrived meanwhile;
.IP \(bu 3
two consumers watching the same thing each get their own copy, and one
acknowledging does not take the event from the other, so no queue per
consumer is needed;
.IP \(bu 3
two instances of one consumer share the work by sharing a name, since a
claim is atomic and exactly one of them gets any given event.
.RE
.PP
A filter is a JSON object of payload fields that must match exactly, and
it is evaluated when the event is published rather than when it is
delivered \- so a subscriber's backlog is proportional to what it asked
for, not to how busy the installation is. ESM's object events carry
.IR bucketName ,
.IR key ,
.I prefix
and
.I directory
for this purpose:
.I prefix
is what makes "this directory" expressible, and
.I directory
false keeps out the zero\-byte markers an FTP
.B MKD
leaves behind.
.PP
Subscriptions are scoped to the caller's account. An event whose payload
names a different account is never stored for a subscriber at all, which
is why account\-scoped events cannot leak across a shared installation.
.SH DELIVERY
A subscription is either
.I durable
(the default) or
.IR live .
.PP
A durable subscription stores every matching event and keeps it until
the subscriber acknowledges it. A live one stores nothing: its events
are pushed to whatever sessions are attached and are then gone, which
costs no database write and is what a view - a UI showing a bucket -
actually wants.
.PP
Either way, a client that holds a websocket to the gateway is told the
moment something happens rather than asking on a timer. A subscribe
frame on that connection is an EES subscription \- the same names,
filters and modes this module takes over HTTP \- so a stream is
described once and can be attached to from anywhere:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"type":"subscribe","id":"1","name":"invoice-import",
 "topic":"esm.object.created","filter":{"bucketName":"inbox"}}

{"type":"subscribe","id":"2","name":"invoice-import"}

{"type":"subscribe","id":"3","topic":"esm.object.created"}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The first registers a subscription and attaches this connection to it,
exactly as
.BR euclid-cli-ees-subscribe-events (1)
would; a named subscription defaults to durable. The second names one
and says no more, which attaches without redefining it \- the way to
listen to a subscription somebody else set up. The third names nobody:
the gateway makes a subscription for the connection, tells the client
its name in the ack, keeps it live, and removes it when the connection
ends, because a subscription that only one socket knows about cannot
outlive it.
.PP
Unsubscribing follows the same shape: with a topic it removes that event
type from the subscription, with only a name it detaches this connection
and leaves the subscription \- and whatever a durable one has collected
\- alone.
.PP
What arrives then differs by mode, and the difference is deliberate. A
live session receives the event itself, since nothing else will ever
carry it. A durable session receives only a notice that something is
waiting - two instances share a name, and both acting on one event is
exactly what the claim below prevents - so it claims with
.B receive-events
and the store decides which instance gets it.
.PP
.B receive-events
therefore stops being the normal path for a connected client and becomes
what it should be: catching up after a disconnect, and the way in for
clients that cannot hold a socket open.
.SH KEEPING UP
A connection that stops reading is not allowed to grow the gateway without
limit: each one has a bounded outbox
.RI ( euclid.gateway.websocket.max\-outbox\-frames
and
.IR \-bytes ),
and when it fills, pushed events are dropped rather than responses \- a
response has a caller blocked on it, while an event a durable subscriber
misses is still in the store. The client is then sent
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"type":"lag","dropped":12}
.fi
.RE
.PP
once it starts reading again. For a durable subscription that is an
instruction rather than a loss: call
.B receive-events
and claim what was missed. For a live one it is what it says \- those
events are gone, which is the bargain a live subscription makes.
.PP
In the other direction, a burst does not become a burst of notices. A
durable subscriber is told once and then not again until
.I euclid.eventbus.notify\-interval\-ms
has passed, so purging a bucket of ten thousand objects wakes a listener a
handful of times rather than ten thousand \- a notice carries no payload,
so collapsing them loses nothing. The first one is always sent
immediately, so a single event is never delayed by the interval.
.SH CLAIMS
.B receive-events
does not delete anything: it takes a lease. A claimed event is invisible
to every other claimer until its visibility timeout runs out, and comes
back if it is never acknowledged \- which is what makes a consumer
crashing halfway through its work harmless. Acknowledging with
.B ack-events
is what finally deletes it.
.PP
An event that is never claimed is kept for seven days and then expires,
so a consumer that never comes back cannot fill the database.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B subscribe-events
Registers a durable subscription for a name, or narrows an existing one.
See
.BR euclid-cli-ees-subscribe-events (1).
.TP
.B unsubscribe-events
Removes a subscription, and the events still waiting for it. See
.BR euclid-cli-ees-unsubscribe-events (1).
.TP
.B list-subscriptions
Shows what a subscriber is subscribed to and how much is waiting. See
.BR euclid-cli-ees-list-subscriptions (1).
.TP
.B receive-events
Claims a subscriber's waiting events, optionally long\-polling for one.
See
.BR euclid-cli-ees-receive-events (1).
.TP
.B ack-events
Acknowledges claimed events, which deletes them. See
.BR euclid-cli-ees-ack-events (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I ees
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty " " \fIBOOL\fR
Pretty-print the JSON response. Defaults to \fItrue\fR.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signature " " \fISCHEME\fR
Signature scheme used for signed service calls: \fIrfc9421\fR (the
default) or \fIsigv4\fR for AWS Signature Version 4.
Overrides
.I euclid.cli.signature
from the configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the euclid-cli version and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.B ees
commands are authenticated using the session created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials .
.PP
Unlike
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1)
or
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
no action here is administrator-only. A subscriber only ever sees events
of its own account, and the principal that needs to subscribe is usually
an application's own technical user rather than an operator.
.PP
Requesting help for an action does not require a session, so
.B "euclid-cli ees <action> \-\-help"
works before logging in.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, there is no session, the request could not be
sent, or the server rejected it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
JSON file holding the current session's bearer token and/or access
key, created by
.B euclid-cli eam login
and read by every
.B ees
action.
.SH EXAMPLES
Watch one bucket's uploads, ignoring directory markers, and drain what
has arrived:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees subscribe-events \e
    --name invoice-import \e
    --event-types esm.object.created,esm.object.deleted \e
    --filter '{"bucketName":"inbox","directory":false}'

euclid-cli ees receive-events --name invoice-import --wait-time 20
euclid-cli ees ack-events --name invoice-import --event-ids <id>,<id>
.fi
.RE
